<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Hayek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich August von Hayek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Hayek was an Austrian economist and philosopher of the Austrian school of economics, known for his contributions to political economy, political philosophy and intellectual history. He earned doctorates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, became a British national in 1938, and taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and Freiburg. His most popular work, The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> (1944), has been republished many times, and his account of how prices communicate information led to his share of the 1974 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences with Gunnar Myrdal. He rejected the label conservative, preferring classical liberal or libertarian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek.jpg" alt="Friedrich Hayek" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was DickClarkMises at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Hayek_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Hayek</b> and Milton Friedman held that central planning by an unchecked state bred inefficiency and stagnation, and the Hungarian economist János Kornai analysed the chronic shortages of the planned economies.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Hayek</b> argued in The Road to Serfdom (1944) that the market is the only way to decide what to produce and how to distribute it without coercion, and Milton Friedman that centralised economies are always accompanied by political repression.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Hayek"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
